#!/bin/sh
# Refuses a commit that would put a secret into git history.
#
# WHY THIS IS THE ONLY THING HERE. A pre-commit hook that runs the test suite gets
# turned off within a week, because it makes every commit slow and most commits do not
# need it — and a hook people turn off protects nothing. So this runs exactly one check,
# it takes well under a second, and it guards the one mistake that a later commit cannot
# fix: once a database or a credential is in history, deleting the file changes nothing.
# Everything else (syntax, static checks, tests, browser checks) runs in CI, where being
# slow is fine and being late is survivable.
#
# TO ENABLE (once per checkout — git will not use a committed hooks directory on its own,
# by design, since cloning a repository must never run its code):
#
#     git config core.hooksPath .githooks
#
# TO BYPASS in a genuine emergency: `git commit --no-verify`. If you find yourself doing
# that routinely, the scanner has a false positive and the scanner is what needs fixing —
# see the no-false-positives note in backend/scan-secrets.js.
exec node backend/scan-secrets.js --staged
